From 666a6195455b80232265d29fcd2a54e23a054c0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 16:16:06 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 14/44] fix(chexc): review findings F1-F5; clause changes now
 spread incrementally

The one that matters (F1, grown): a capability-clause-only change on a
declaration was invisible to incremental compilation -- for 'performs' AND,
it turned out, for 'throws' all along. Two hashes exist: HashMemberSignature
(which had 'throws' from the start and gains 'performs' here) feeds the
virtual/API hashes, while DeclHash.sig -- the value the pollution analysis
spreads on -- comes from ASTHasher::SigHash, which hashed no function clauses
at all. A clause-only change therefore classified as a 'body' change and
spreading stopped at the declaration itself: callers kept their stale
obligations silently. Diagnosed from .cached decision logs ('risky: body',
spreading list without the caller); ledger C2 was only ever closed in the
caller-changed direction, which is the only direction incr_caps tested.

Both clauses now enter SigHash, with a comment explaining the two-hash split
so the next fix lands in the right one. Verified: the callee-clause-change
probe now errors incrementally, and the spreading log lists the caller.

The rest of the review batch:
- F2: clause order (proposal 9.1 rule 8) is enforced -- 'throws E performs H'
  was silently accepted through the two ParseCapabilityClauses call sites; now
  diagnosed at the offending clause on both the declaration and functional-type
  paths, and parsed anyway so semantic checking still sees the requirements.
  Accessors now share the same helper.
- F3: an effect miss is reported as 'Handler<C>', not 'CanThrow<C>' (proposal
  8.2 naming). The demand carries an isEffect flag computed at the miss site;
  the collecting handler consumes the same flag instead of re-deriving it.
- F4: a duplicate 'performs' clause gets its own wording.
- F5: PrintNode prints 'performsClause' on FuncBody and FuncType.

Tests: performs_order_neg and incr_performs verified red first (each exposed
its bug live -- the order test compiled clean, the incremental test exited 0
with no diagnostic); incr_caps2 pins the throws twin of the incremental
direction; performs_xpkg{,_neg} and performs_generic{,_neg} close the two
review coverage gaps and were green on arrival. CheckedExceptions +
IncrementalCompilation 2025/2025; ctest 49/49.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
